     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>I&#39;m trying to fit a model <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/lavaan/bUlPLQXHFto\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/forum/%23!topic/lavaan/bUlPLQXHFto&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792424998482000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1xFYcQo8U1BQ1b6sxV-gSL\">(previously described here) and I&#39;m getting a bunch of warnings:</a></div><div><br></div><div><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:9pt!important;outline:none;border:none;word-break:break-all;margin:0px;white-space:pre-wrap!important;line-height:14px;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:-webkit-left;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">Warning messages:\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">1: </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">In lav_data_full(data = data, group = group, cluster = cluster,  :\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"> lavaan WARNING: small number of observations (nobs &lt; nvar)\r\n  nobs = 93 nvar = 112\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">2: </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">In lav_samplestats_step2(UNI = FIT, ov.names = ov.names, zero.add = zero.add,  :\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"> lavaan WARNING: correlation between variables q0069_0010par and q0051_0007par is (nearly) 1.0\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">3: </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">In lav_model_vcov(lavmodel = lavmodel, lavsamplestats = lavsamplestats,  :\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"> lavaan WARNING:\r\n    The variance-covariance matrix of the estimated parameters (vcov)\r\n    does not appear to be positive definite! The smallest eigenvalue\r\n    (= 9.507439e-16) is close to zero. This may be a symptom that the\r\n    model is not identified.</span></span></pre></div><div><br></div><div>The model I&#39;m trying to fit is fairly big, so I&#39;m putting it in a separate post below.</div><div><br></div><div>My main concern is whether any of the above warnings imply that the resulting estimates are wrong in some big way. I can get error-free fits with certain subsets of factors and the results remain consistent all the way to the the full model, so it seems to me like the estimates should still be usable. And if not, I&#39;d like to know what changes would be needed to be made to remedy this.<br></div><div><br></div><div><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:9pt!important;outline:none;border:none;word-break:break-all;margin:0px;white-space:pre-wrap!important;line-height:14px;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:-webkit-left;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span><span style=\"outline:none;border:none;word-break:break-all;margin:0px;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><br></span></span></pre></div></div>"
