  {
   "id": "dh_bv1jABgAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2018-08-24T08:03:23Z",
   "subject": "Re: Is a model still usable if there are highly correlated indicators, low sample, negative eigenvalues?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">The warning messages are pretty clear.  You cannot trust results when you have more variables than observations.  I&#39;m surprised the model would even run.  Results are already questionable when you estimate more parameters than you have observations, and there are certainly more model parameters than variables (at a minimum, there is one (residual) variance per variable, plus parameters representing relationships between variables).  Furthermore, you have categorical data.  Simulations seem to indicate that even when you have more observations than parameters (and certainly more than variables), estimates still do not stabilize until you have moderately large samples (e.g., <i>N</i> &gt;= 500 or 1000).<div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/10705511.2014.859510\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/10705511.2014.859510&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792424998485000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3lPxXKbYn_3oQN555wRJhZ\">http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/10705511.2014.859510</a></div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/10705511.2014.882658\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/10705511.2014.882658&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792424998485000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0KFMnGZj-HFQ1JSUzZaj1H\">http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/10705511.2014.882658</a></div><div><br></div><div>Sorry to say it, but if I were reviewing your paper, I would not even be able to recommend a revision unless you could increase your sample size by an order of magnitude to be able to fit a model this large.</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Postdoctoral Researcher, Methods and Statistics</div><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div style=\"font-size:13px\">UvA web page: <a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792424998485000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2DoJzzsyW5PdiYUUaqxtia\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div><div><br></div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "esRhzVvVBgAJ",
   "from": "J V",
   "author": "c1d90967b611a5b6",
   "date": "2018-08-24T14:28:26Z",
   "subject": "Re: Is a model still usable if there are highly correlated indicators, low sample, negative eigenvalues?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Oh dear. Thanks for the references.</div><div><br></div><div>I realize I don&#39;t have enough sample size to reliably test even a fraction of the factors of interest, but I see they all review ML-based estimators. Do you know if the same hard limitations would apply using Bayesian estimation? This is more of a pilot study so even if parameter posteriors are very wide, some approximation of their location would be useful.<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br>On Friday, 24 August 2018 03:03:23 UTC-5, Terrence Jorgensen  wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">The warning messages are pretty clear.  You cannot trust results when you have more variables than observations.  I&#39;m surprised the model would even run.  Results are already questionable when you estimate more parameters than you have observations, and there are certainly more model parameters than variables (at a minimum, there is one (residual) variance per variable, plus parameters representing relationships between variables).  Furthermore, you have categorical data.  Simulations seem to indicate that even when you have more observations than parameters (and certainly more than variables), estimates still do not stabilize until you have moderately large samples (e.g., <i>N</i> &gt;= 500 or 1000).<div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/10705511.2014.859510\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/10705511.2014.859510&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792424998486000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1JMEJiZwCdVcxZzZya6Itv\">http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/10705511.2014.859510</a></div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/10705511.2014.882658\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/10705511.2014.882658&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792424998486000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1EWO-IvBhCIBhQFtuXak3z\">http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/10705511.2014.882658</a></div><div><br></div><div>Sorry to say it, but if I were reviewing your paper, I would not even be able to recommend a revision unless you could increase your sample size by an order of magnitude to be able to fit a model this large.</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Postdoctoral Researcher, Methods and Statistics</div><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div style=\"font-size:13px\">UvA web page: <a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792424998486000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3vXPYVvvMNAj1qpTF6sIIf\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div><div><br></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
