   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2018-08-25T12:36:03Z",
   "subject": "Re: Is a model still usable if there are highly correlated indicators, low sample, negative eigenvalues?",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Do you know if the same hard limitations would apply using Bayesian estimation? </div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Unlike these frequentist methods, Bayes is not built on asymptotic theory, although more data always helps to draw more confident conclusions.  If there are convergence problems with MCMC estimation, you can help it out by specifying more informative priors, but make sure to perform a sensitivity analysis with different priors to see how much your results depend on them.  Hopefully you will find the blavaan package helpful.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Postdoctoral Researcher, Methods and Statistics</div><div style=\"font-size:13px\">Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div style=\"font-size:13px\">UvA web page: <a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792424998486000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3vXPYVvvMNAj1qpTF6sIIf\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div><div><br></div></div></div></div>"
